 do with the Obama  administration especially and so you  know he caught calling Valerie Jarrett  uh you know of um a p-- is just what  you're going to get with that yeah man I  think that anybody who was sort of like  surprised by the comment was just not  paying attention to Rosanna for the last  many many years and I want to get back  to the mental illness component it's in  a second to talk about this from ABC's  perspective my analysis from day one was  that neither the reboot of the series  nor the firing of Roseanne has any  connection to any sort of moral or  ethical value judgment of the show nor  of anything that Roseanne did it's been  just business decisions all along the  reboot as you mentioned was out of this  idea that it could be great business to  do a show that seems to sort of be  sympathetic to the down-home Trump  supporting good old whatever of Middle  America and the firing also was what  they assessed to be the best business  decision at the time but to ascribe any  morality to it I I think is wrong unless  you think otherwise no I don't I mean  certainly you can't perceive any of that  maybe there was some sort of buried  moral trigger in the black hearts of ABC  executives that was finally dredged up  by this last tweet but you know they  there was the audio of them ABC  executives in an advertising meeting  they were they were joking about how  Trump's or sorry  Roseanne's tweets were so incredibly  offensive and weird so yeah I think it  was it was a purely business decision  there was just a calculation of you know  ratings versus diluting the brand equity  or sparking a potential boycott for  liberals you know it's like at a certain  point you didn't want to cut your losses  but yeah there was no morality here the  Mental Illness thing I mean the you  pointed out some of the the ideas that  Roseanne has has parroted retweeted over  the last many years it includes you know  CIA mind control conspiracy theory stuff  about George Soros involved in almost  every area of life that at some point  somebody has suggested and I think that  that's something that is either often  missed or sort of miscalculated when we  think about some of these incidents that  happen publicly I mean the the pattern  is this sort of paranoid delusions that  seem to have been an underlying theme of  a lot of the political conspiracies that  she subscribed to how should we as the  public account for the fact that whether  it's Roseanne or somebody else there do  sometimes seem to be significant mental  illness components to these implosion x'  that take place publicly I mean how  should the general public account for  that in our reaction you know that's a  really hard question it's you know I  mean the the the question of assigning  morale you know liability to someone who  may not be in full possession of you  know their faculties I feel like it's  it's kind of a case-by-case basis you  know because you know there are lots and  lots of different kinds of mental  illness people are you know some of them  are mild and don't interfere with  people's ability to reason while others  you know this can be totally  debilitating where people are are not  even you know coherent at all  this seems like kind of somewhere in the  middle you know I I it certainly to me  seems like there is an and kind of an  uncomfortable element of exploitation in  this and that Roseanne you know  she she does by all accounts have  serious mental illness that may not be  being treated properly and her wild  antics get tons of attention and ABC you  know clearly knew about that and was  capitalizing on it and you know what she  may have needed was not this kind of  immense at level of celebrity and  attention and more like you know of some  therapy and and Gianna treatment of some  sort but on the other hand you know I  mean it doesn't I mean obviously I don't  know Rosanna personally I've never met  her or anything like that but she  doesn't seem like someone who is  completely senseless you know she can  still speak and she certainly seems to  have you know honestly held awful views  about you know black people and Arabs  and Muslims and and you know George  Soros so I don't know you know  ultimately I guess it would I would come  down and somewhere in the middle  thinking that you know Rosanna is not  without blame but it's it's a bit of an  exculpatory factor being unwell in that  way you've in addition to writing about  Roseanne and we're gonna link to your  article about that you've also talked  about the intellectual dark web  so-called intellectual dark web a bit  and this sort of free speech warrior  movement that is out there and I think  that that's interesting in this story  because there is a group of people that  like to or at least first analyze  stories through the lens of the  so-called battle of ideas which I have a  hard time even saying non-city really at  this point or through the lens of free  speech and censorship and I found it  interesting that some of the individuals  who last week applauded the decision of  the National Football League to say you  now are not allowed to sit or  Neal or or not stand basically during  the national anthem if you're gonna be  on the field before a football game and  applauded that decision are saying that  the decision now made by ABC to fire  Roseanne is somehow an impingement on  her free speech or censorship of  comments that she made outside of her  employment relationship with ABC or  whatever fundamentally aren't these just  both cases of private businesses making  decisions that they calculate are the  right business decisions for them and  that I guess let me couch the question  this way is there any way you can even  conceive of in which one might have a  different opinion of the NFL decision  and the ABC opinion are there any  material differences I would say it's a  very it's very similar in one sense in  that yes you know these are these are  private businesses I think that you  could make a you could make a tolerable  sort of constitutional argument that you  know this that the case of the NFL  players you know not then they're not  saying anything in you know any sort of  explicit way but just you know sort of  is this like passive demonstration is it  is constitutionally protected speech in  a way that just like egregiously  offending the you know your bosses it's  not but you know I mean it I think it  goes to the heart though of the totally  unserious way that this so-called  intellectual dark web issue has been  treated you know you you have a bunch of  people who you know this Jordan Pederson  and and Dave Rubin and so forth who are  being set up you know as these like  victims of  of oppression but there's no real kind  of attempt to articulate the differences  you know and and systematically  investigate the issue of suppressed  speech which is absolutely rampant in  this country and usually it you know the  worst cases all usually involves stuff  like Palestinian BDS activists and so  forth  they've literally lots of attempts to  make that kind of speech illegal and not  just like people yell at you on Twitter  but yeah you know it it certainly you  know that this  I think the Roseann case was  particularly illustrative because this  would be a case where you could buy the  lights of the kind of intellectual dark  web people you know they ought to have  raised the exact same stink over that as  they have over you know other instances  of people being mean people losing their  jobs but in this case the comment was so  racist that there you know there was  just if it was you know indefensible and  I think it goes to the heart of what  these that this the Barry Weiss and so  forth are actually doing which is  constructing tendentious definitional  sort of fortresses around their  preferred positions and not any sort of  rigorous defense of free speech as such  yeah it seems that the free speech  compo